"A transaction schedule diagram shows interleaved reads, writes, commits, and aborts to analyze concurrency behavior.\n\nUse when: Explain lost updates, write skew, or isolation anomalies. Review whether an observed history matches the promised isolation level.\n\nAvoid when: Only the final table structure or service topology is relevant.\n\nVisual grammar: Parallel lanes identify transactions. Ordered markers show reads, writes, commits, and aborts on named items. Read-from or conflict edges are optional but use explicit semantics.\n\nBuild: Choose a minimal history and state the database isolation model. Place operations in their observed or hypothetical order. Annotate the values read and versions written using synthetic examples where appropriate. Route conflict edges between lanes without crossing operation labels.\n\nCheck: Each read is tied to the version it can legally observe. Transaction boundaries and commit outcomes are explicit. The claimed anomaly follows from the actual schedule and isolation semantics. Value annotations and dependency arrows remain visually separate.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a possible interleaving as evidence it occurred in production. Assuming serializable means transactions execute one at a time.\n\nExample: Two transactions each read an available-seat count before writing separate reservations, exposing the stated concurrency assumption.\n\nAlternatives: Sequence diagram, Petri net."
